course: dictionary
lesson: 3174
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. Employment has three main meanings: having a paid job, giving someone work, and using something. Look at the words around it before you choose.
explain: «An employment tribunal is a kind of court for work disputes, such as being sacked unfairly or not being paid what is owed.» The clues are sacked, unfairly and not being paid. They show that this is about problems at work, so employment is linked to jobs.
explain: Some phrases help too. «In employment» means having a paid job, and «seek employment» means looking for a job. In maths and geography, employment often means how many people have jobs, so you may see it with numbers or percentages.
fact: Employment means having a paid job.
fact: Employment can also mean giving someone work, or the use of something (this is more formal).
fact: «An employment tribunal» is a kind of court for work disputes, such as being sacked unfairly or not being paid what is owed.
fact: «In employment» means having a paid job, and «seek employment» means looking for work.
fact: Employment is usually uncountable, so we say «employment» for jobs in general, not «an employment».
exercise: «She has been in employment since she left school.» What does employment mean here? | Giving a speech in class | Using a tool | *Having a paid job | Going to court
exercise: In geography, an employment rate tells us what? | How many children go to school | How tall the buildings are | How far a town is from the sea | *How many people have jobs
exercise: Which phrase means looking for a job? | employment rate | full-time employment | employment tribunal | *seek employment
test: «An employment tribunal» is most like which of these? | A club for sports fans | A library for old books | A place where food is cooked | *A court that settles problems about work
test: In «the employment of new machines», what does employment mean? | A kind of court | Having a paid job | Giving a speech | *The use of something
exam: What does «the employment of ten new staff» mean? | Ten staff leaving the company | Ten new computers being bought | Ten people having no jobs | *Giving ten people work
